      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what she has read, where she found it, who wrote it, and what it was about the diet that appealed to her.\n\nAsk what has happened in the eight months since her diagnosis, how she has found it, and what she feels she has any control over at the moment.\n\nAcknowledge directly why doing something feels better than doing nothing, and say that this is a reasonable thing to want.\n\nExplain honestly what is known about diet and this condition: where there is evidence, where there is none, and be specific rather than dismissive.\n\nAssess the diet nutritionally: what it removes, what deficiencies it risks, and what it would take to follow it safely.\n\nSeparate the parts that are harmless or helpful from the parts that are not, and be clear about which is which.\n\nExplain what does have evidence for her: vitamin D, weight, cardiovascular risk, and a generally good-quality diet — and be honest that none of these is a cure.\n\nAsk what she would do if she followed it for a year and nothing changed, and talk about what that would cost her.\n\nAsk what her neurology team have said about it, and offer to write to them so that everyone is working from the same information.\n\nAgree what she is going to do, make clear you will support her either way, and arrange monitoring if she goes ahead so that the risks are watched rather than ignored."
