      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what she is eating on a typical day, in detail, rather than asking what she has cut out.\n\nAsk what improved, when it improved, and what has happened since, since the timing matters more than the list.\n\nAsk about the symptoms she has now: the fatigue, the hair, feeling cold, her periods, and her sleep.\n\nBe honest about the evidence: what is known about diet and inflammatory arthritis, what is not, and which parts of her protocol have some basis and which have none.\n\nExplain what six food groups removed at once does to her intake — energy, protein, calcium, iron, B vitamins and fibre — and link each one to a symptom she has described.\n\nExplain what you would like to test and why, and what you would want her medical team to know.\n\nRebuild rather than forbid: propose reintroducing one group at a time, with the order chosen by her, and explain how to tell whether a reintroduction has actually caused anything.\n\nOffer the parts of the diet that do have evidence and that she can keep, so that she is not being asked to give up everything she has learned.\n\nAsk whether she has changed anything about her arthritis medication since starting the diet, since that would change how you interpret the six weeks of improvement.\n\nAgree what she will eat this week, agree the first reintroduction, and arrange a review that is close enough to matter."
