      "candidateCard": "Ask her what she has been seeing at visiting times over the last fortnight, and what worries her most about what she has seen.\n\nAsk what she has been told so far, and by whom, and correct anything that is plainly wrong.\n\nExplain honestly what happens to eating in advanced dementia, and that reduced intake is part of the illness rather than a failure of care.\n\nExplain what a feeding tube would and would not achieve for him: what the evidence shows about survival, about pneumonia, about pressure sores and about comfort.\n\nExplain the burdens honestly too: the procedure, the risk, and the likelihood of it being pulled out and of restraint being considered.\n\nDeal with the word \"starving\" directly rather than avoiding it, and explain the difference between withholding food and a body that is no longer able to use it.\n\nExplain what IS being done: careful hand feeding at his pace, the foods he still accepts, mouth care, and the fact that he is offered food at every meal.\n\nGive her a role that matters: mouth care, bringing the things he has always liked, sitting with him at mealtimes.\n\nAsk whether her father ever said anything about how he would want to be cared for, and whether anything was written down.\n\nAgree what happens next, offer to be there when the medical team discuss it, and make clear that no decision is being taken today without the family."
