      "candidateCard": "Ask what he drinks in a day, everything, including tea, soup, milk on cereal, and what he has with his tablets.\n\nAsk about the thirst itself: how bad, when, whether his mouth is dry, and whether it is worse after his tablets.\n\nExplain why the general advice about drinking plenty does not apply to him, using what his own heart is doing rather than a rule.\n\nExplain the link he may not have made: the weight gain, the ankles and the breathlessness are the same problem, and the daily weight is the earliest warning of it.\n\nExplain what salt does to thirst, so that the salt restriction becomes a way of being less thirsty rather than a second deprivation.\n\nGive the practical measures for thirst: ice cubes, frozen fruit, sugar-free chewing gum, mouth rinses, smaller cups, spreading the allowance across the day, and taking tablets with food.\n\nAsk when he takes his diuretic and whether the timing is driving him to drink, and whether he has been checked for diabetes recently.\n\nSet up the daily weighing: same scales, same time, written down, and the exact figure at which he should telephone the team.\n\nAgree what he will do this week, agree one salt change and one fluid change rather than both at once, and be honest that this is difficult rather than pretending it is not."
