    "subTest": "SPEAKING",
    "taskType": "SPEAKING_ROLEPLAY",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "title": "Dietetics — A pregnant woman following a vegan diet",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Establish in the first minute that you are not going to ask her to stop. Assess what she actually eats, identify the specific nutrients that need attention in pregnancy, and give a plan that works within her diet rather than against it.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Antenatal dietetic clinic, 14 weeks' gestation.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the dietitian.",
      "patientRole": "A 29-year-old woman, vegan for six years, referred by her midwife with a haemoglobin of 102 and a booking B12 that has not yet been checked. She has come expecting to be told to eat meat.",
      "patientConcern": "She has been questioned about her diet at every appointment and is defensive before you have said anything.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Say plainly at the start that a well-planned vegan diet is suitable in pregnancy and that you are not going to ask her to change it.\n\nAsk what she eats on a typical day, and ask it as an assessment rather than as a test.\n\nAsk what supplements she is already taking, in what dose, and for how long.\n\nGo through the nutrients that need attention specifically in pregnancy on this diet: B12, iron, iodine, omega-3, vitamin D, calcium, protein and zinc.\n\nDeal with B12 properly: that a supplement is essential rather than optional, the dose, and that fortified foods alone are usually not enough.\n\nDeal with the iron: her haemoglobin, the plant sources, what helps absorption and what blocks it, and when a supplement is needed.\n\nCover iodine, which is the one most often missed on a vegan diet and which matters for the baby's brain.\n\nGive practical food examples rather than nutrient names — what to add at each meal — and check they are foods she likes and can afford.\n\nAsk about the practical things that change in pregnancy: nausea and what she has been able to keep down, any food aversions, and whether the cost of the fortified products is manageable.\n\nAsk what she has been told at previous appointments and whether anything about it made her reluctant to come today.\n\nAgree what bloods you will ask the midwife to arrange, and agree a review that follows the results rather than a fixed date."
    }
