    "subTest": "SPEAKING",
    "taskType": "SPEAKING_ROLEPLAY",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "title": "Dietetics — A woman convinced she has a food intolerance",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Deal with the test honestly but without humiliating her. Check the red flags first, explain what the test actually measures, and offer the approach that does have evidence — while making clear that her symptoms are real.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Community dietetic clinic, a first appointment.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the dietitian.",
      "patientRole": "A 42-year-old woman with bloating, wind and altered bowel habit for two years. She has paid for a private \"food intolerance\" blood test which reported 23 foods, and she has excluded all of them for four months with no improvement.",
      "patientConcern": "She has spent a considerable amount of money on the test and will hear criticism of it as criticism of her.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask about the symptoms first: how long they have been going on, the pattern across a day and a week, what makes them better and worse, and what they stop her doing.\n\nCheck the things that must not be missed: weight loss, blood in the stool, waking at night with symptoms, a family history of bowel cancer or coeliac disease, and her age at onset.\n\nAsk whether she has been tested for coeliac disease, and whether she was eating gluten at the time.\n\nAsk what she is eating now, after four months of excluding 23 foods, and what that has cost her nutritionally and socially.\n\nExplain what the test she paid for actually measures and what that antibody indicates, honestly, and without implying that she was foolish to try it.\n\nSay clearly that her symptoms are real and that you are not suggesting otherwise.\n\nExplain what does have evidence for her symptoms: the first-line dietary and lifestyle measures, and then a properly supervised exclusion approach if those fail.\n\nBe explicit that a supervised exclusion is a diagnostic process with a reintroduction phase, and that excluding foods permanently without testing them is what has left her where she is.\n\nAsk what she is most afraid the symptoms mean, since two years of bloating with no explanation usually carries a fear that nobody has asked about.\n\nAgree what she will reintroduce first, agree the record she will keep, and arrange the review."
    }
