    "subTest": "SPEAKING",
    "taskType": "SPEAKING_ROLEPLAY",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "title": "Dietetics — A supplement given despite a recorded allergy",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Say what happened before she asks, apologise for your own error without diffusing it across the team, explain what has been done, and be clear about the investigation and her right to complain.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Hospital ward, at the bedside with the curtains closed.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the ward dietitian. You prescribed the supplement.",
      "patientRole": "A 58-year-old woman with a documented milk allergy who was given a milk-based supplement drink for two days and had abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhoea. She has not had a severe reaction, and she has now worked out why she was unwell.",
      "patientConcern": "She told two members of staff about the allergy on admission and it is in her notes; she feels she was not listened to.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Tell her plainly what happened: which drink, that it contained milk protein, that her allergy was recorded, and that it was prescribed by you.\n\nApologise without qualification and without spreading the responsibility across the ward.\n\nAcknowledge that she told two people on admission and that it is in her notes, and that she was right, before she has to say it.\n\nAsk how she has been: the pain, the vomiting, the diarrhoea, when it started and whether anything is still happening.\n\nExplain what has already been done: the drink stopped, her allergy status re-flagged, the medical team informed and her observations checked.\n\nExplain what she will be given instead and how it is different, and check she is willing to try it.\n\nExplain what happens now that this has been reported: the incident review, what it looks at, and that its purpose is to find how a recorded allergy was missed.\n\nTell her she can have a written account and how to raise a formal complaint, and give her the details without being asked.\n\nAsk whether she would like her family told, and by whom, and whether she wants somebody with her when this is discussed again.\n\nAsk what would make her feel safer for the rest of this admission, and do that specific thing before you leave the bedside."
    }
