      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Find out what he is most worried about, and what he has already been told by his family, before you give your own view.\n\nDescribe what you actually found on examination — the chest, the throat, the ears, the temperature and how the child is behaving — so that your conclusion has visible reasons behind it.\n\nExplain in plain words why this pattern is viral, how long a cough like this normally lasts, and why a fever on day four is not in itself a sign of a bacterial infection.\n\nExplain what antibiotics would and would not do here, including the effects the child would be exposed to for no benefit, without lecturing him about resistance in general.\n\nSay what would change your mind, specifically: what you would want to see or hear, and by when.\n\nGive him the safety net in concrete terms: the signs that mean he should seek help the same day, including breathing difficulty, drowsiness, poor fluid intake, a rash that does not fade, or a fever lasting beyond five days.\n\nExplain what he can do for the child's comfort tonight and tomorrow, with doses and timings.\n\nDeal with the wedding directly: what he can reasonably expect tomorrow, and what to do if things change while he is away from home.\n\nCheck what he has understood by asking him what he would do if the child became drowsy."
