      "patientConcern": "She assumed the appointment was to be told her chest was clear, and has told her husband to wait in the car.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Check what she was expecting from today's appointment and what she has been thinking since the x-ray, before you say anything about the result.\n\nAsk whether she would like her husband to come in from the car, and wait if she says yes.\n\nGive the finding in one plain sentence early in the conversation, then stop and give her time before you add anything else.\n\nExplain what the report says and what it does not say — that it shows an area that should not be there, and that it cannot tell you what that area is.\n\nAnswer honestly if she asks whether it is cancer: that it is one of the possibilities, that other causes are possible, and that the tests are how that question gets answered.\n\nExplain what happens next in concrete terms: the urgent referral, the two-week timescale, the CT scan, and that she will probably be offered a test to take a sample.\n\nAsk what she wants to know now and what she would rather leave until the results, and follow her lead rather than completing your own list.\n\nCheck how she is getting home, whether she is alone tonight, and who she can talk to.\n\nAgree how you will stay in contact and give her a way to reach you before the appointment comes."
