    "title": "Medicine — Explaining a medication error",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "This is a duty of candour conversation. Tell her what happened before she asks again, apologise on behalf of the team without blaming a named person, explain the actual effect and what is being done, and tell her about the formal investigation and her right to a written account.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Hospital ward, at the bedside with the curtains closed, on the day the error was found.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the doctor responsible for the patient's care today. The error was made by the ward team, not by you personally.",
      "patientRole": "A 58-year-old woman who has received twice the intended dose of her blood pressure medication for three days, because a dose was transcribed incorrectly on admission. She has had two dizzy episodes and one near-fall.",
      "patientConcern": "She has connected the dizziness with the tablets herself and had already asked twice whether something was wrong.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Tell her at the start, plainly, that an error has been made in her medication, and say what the error was before you explain anything else.\n\nApologise clearly and without qualification, on behalf of the team, and without naming or blaming an individual member of staff.\n\nAcknowledge that she asked twice whether something was wrong and that she was right, and say so explicitly rather than passing over it.\n\nExplain what the drug does at that dose, what the dizziness was, and what harm has and has not occurred as far as you can tell.\n\nExplain what has already been done: the dose corrected, the observations, the blood tests, and any monitoring that will continue over the next day or two.\n\nExplain what will happen now that this has been reported: the incident review, what it is for, and that its purpose is to change the system rather than to punish somebody.\n\nTell her she is entitled to a written account of what happened, and offer to arrange it.\n\nAsk what questions she has, and be honest about anything you do not yet know rather than filling the gap with reassurance.\n\nAsk whether she would like her family told, and by whom, and agree who will speak to her again tomorrow."
    }
