      "setting": "General practice, an appointment booked to discuss test results.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the general practitioner. All the tests are normal.",
      "patientRole": "A 29-year-old man who has attended eleven times in six months with different symptoms — headaches, chest tightness, a lump in the throat, and pins and needles. He has had blood tests, an ECG and a brain scan, all normal. He now believes something has been missed.",
      "patientConcern": "His father died suddenly of a heart attack at 34, and he is now 29; he does not connect this with his symptoms.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Give the results plainly and specifically — which tests, and what each one has ruled out — rather than saying only that everything is fine.\n\nSay clearly that you believe his symptoms are real, and that \"normal tests\" does not mean \"imagining it\".\n\nAsk him what he has been most afraid the tests would show, and let him answer fully.\n\nAsk about his father: what happened, how old he was, and what he was told at the time — and ask when he last thought about it.\n\nAsk what he notices about when the symptoms come on: the time of day, what he is doing, and whether anything makes them better or worse.\n\nExplain how the body produces exactly these symptoms when it is in a state of alarm, describing the mechanism rather than labelling him.\n\nBe honest about what further testing would achieve, and about the harm of repeating tests that are already normal.\n\nOffer what would actually help: talking therapy, what it involves, how to access it, and what you can do in the meantime.\n\nAsk what he would need to see or hear to feel reassured, since more tests have not achieved it, and be honest if the answer is that no test will.\n\nAgree a plan for the next three months that does not depend on new symptoms bringing him back, and agree what he should do if something genuinely new happens."
