      "candidateCard": "Check what he already understands about the scan and about what the disease is doing, before you add anything.\n\nAsk what he wants to know: whether he wants the numbers, the broad picture, or only what happens next — and follow that.\n\nSay plainly, once, that this treatment cannot cure the cancer, and stop rather than moving on quickly.\n\nSet out both options honestly and side by side: what chemotherapy could offer in time and in symptoms, what it would cost him in side effects and in hospital visits, and what best supportive care would actually involve — which is not nothing.\n\nAnswer his direct question about what you would do without hiding behind neutrality, while making clear that the answer depends on what matters to him.\n\nAsk what he is hoping to be well enough for in the coming months, and give him room to say it.\n\nExplain how the decision can be revisited: that starting does not mean continuing, and that stopping is a decision he can make at any point.\n\nExplain what happens next either way: the review appointment, the palliative care team, and who he can telephone.\n\nCheck what he has taken from the conversation by asking him to tell you, in his own words, what he will say to his wife tonight.\n\nAsk whether he would like his family present when the decision is made, and agree a time to decide rather than pressing him today."
