      "candidateCard": "Check briefly and without repeating the teaching: how the last three changes went, whether she has done one alone, and how her skin looks.\n\nCover the practical safety points that bring people back in: how much she needs to drink, the signs of dehydration, what a high output looks like and when to ring about it, and the foods that block.\n\nExplain what to do about her medicines now that she has an ileostomy, and which tablet forms may not be absorbed.\n\nExplain what supplies she has, how she reorders them, and when the stoma nurse will visit at home.\n\nThen ask her how she is finding it, and give her time to answer rather than filling the silence.\n\nAsk what she is most worried about happening in the first fortnight, and answer that specifically — including leaks, noise, and what she would do if it happened at work.\n\nTalk about telling people: who needs to know, who does not, and what she might say to her employer and to her partner if she wants to.\n\nTell her about the support available — the stoma nurse, the patient association and the local group — and offer to make the first contact for her rather than leaving her a leaflet. Mention that many people find a spare kit in a bag, kept in the car or at work, removes most of the fear of a leak."
