    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Give the truth first, including the parts that reflect badly on the process. Do not promise a time you cannot keep. Deal with the daughter's day, and give her something to hold on to that is within your control.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Medical ward, at the bedside, late morning. She has been dressed and sitting on the bed since eight.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the nurse looking after her today.",
      "patientRole": "A 74-year-old woman who was told yesterday that she would go home today. The pharmacy has not sent her medicines, the transport slot has been missed, and the earliest realistic time is now late afternoon.",
      "patientConcern": "Her daughter has taken the day off work and is waiting at the house, and she believes she is being kept in because the ward wants her bed used for something else.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Tell her plainly, at the start, that she will not be leaving this morning, and say the actual reasons rather than a general apology about delays.\n\nExplain what is missing: which medicines have not arrived, why they must come with her rather than be collected later, and where the request currently is.\n\nExplain the transport situation honestly, including that the earlier slot has gone and what the realistic window now is.\n\nApologise for what this has cost her and her daughter today, without turning the apology into an explanation of how busy the ward is.\n\nCorrect the belief she has formed about the bed, directly and without defensiveness.\n\nTell her exactly what you will do now: chase the pharmacy, ask about an earlier transport slot, and come back to her with a time by a stated hour rather than \"later\".\n\nOffer to telephone her daughter yourself so that she does not have to explain it, and ask whether she would rather do that herself.\n\nDeal with the day as it now is: lunch, a drink, somewhere more comfortable than the edge of the bed, and her tablets due at one o'clock.\n\nAsk her what else she needs before she goes, and check that she knows what her medicines are for and who is visiting her at home tomorrow. Make sure she leaves with the ward's number and knows who to ring if anything is wrong tonight."
    }
