    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Begin by thanking her for saying so. Do not dismiss what she read; put both risks side by side in plain numbers and plain language, and give her a decision she takes part in rather than an instruction she will not follow.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Cardiology day unit, in a side room, at a follow-up appointment.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the nurse specialist reviewing patients who have recently started anticoagulation.",
      "patientRole": "A 66-year-old woman who was started on apixaban three weeks ago for atrial fibrillation. She has taken it for eleven days and then stopped, after reading online about bleeding on the brain, and has not told anyone until today.",
      "patientConcern": "She is more frightened of a bleed she can imagine than of a stroke she cannot, and she expects to be told off for stopping.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Thank her for telling you that she stopped, and make it clear that you would far rather know than not know.\n\nFind out exactly what she read, what she understood by it, and what she was most afraid of, before you offer any information of your own.\n\nExplain in ordinary language what atrial fibrillation does to the blood inside the heart and how a clot from there causes a stroke, so that the reason for the medicine is concrete rather than abstract.\n\nPut the two risks beside each other honestly: the risk of a serious bleed on treatment, and the much larger risk of a stroke without it for someone with her history. Do not pretend the bleeding risk is nothing.\n\nDescribe what a stroke would actually mean for her daily life, because this is the part she has not imagined, and do it without frightening her for its own sake.\n\nExplain the practical safety points: what to do about a cut, dental treatment, over-the-counter painkillers to avoid, and the specific signs of a bleed that mean urgent help.\n\nAsk what would make it possible for her to take it every day, and agree one practical step — a reminder, a dosette box, or a review in two weeks.\n\nAgree what she will do if she becomes frightened again, so that stopping without telling anyone is not the option she reaches for."
    }
