    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Speak to the mother about the plan and to the child about the moment. Explain why the swab matters, offer real choices, and be honest that you will not proceed by force — while making clear what happens to his care if the swab is not done.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Paediatric assessment unit, cubicle with the parent present.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the nurse who needs to take a nasal and throat swab from a four-year-old before he can be moved to a ward bed.",
      "patientRole": "The mother of a four-year-old with a high fever. He screamed through a swab at another hospital three months ago and is now hiding behind her and refusing to open his mouth.",
      "patientConcern": "She is exhausted, feels she is being asked to hold her son down, and is close to refusing the test on his behalf.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Find out what happened at the other hospital and what her son found worst about it, because that detail will decide how you do this.\n\nExplain simply why the swab is needed now: what it is looking for, how the result changes where he is nursed and what treatment he is given, and when the result will come back.\n\nSay plainly that you will not hold him down, and explain what you would do instead if he refuses after everything has been tried.\n\nOffer the choices that are genuinely his: which side of the nose, sitting on his mother's lap or on the bed, counting to three himself, holding the swab first, and having it done by the person he prefers.\n\nExplain to the mother what position helps and why it is a supportive hold rather than a restraint, and check that she is willing before you ask her to do anything.\n\nDescribe honestly what it feels like — a tickle, four seconds, watery eyes — without promising that it will not be unpleasant.\n\nAgree what will happen immediately afterwards, so that there is something on the other side of it.\n\nCheck with her that she agrees to go ahead now, and offer to come back in twenty minutes if she would rather he had time to settle first."
    }
