      "candidateCard": "Ask what he has already been told about smoking and this operation, and how those conversations went, before adding anything of your own.\n\nFind out about his two previous attempts: how long each lasted, what helped, and what he was doing when he started again.\n\nExplain specifically what smoking does to this operation rather than to health in general — the anaesthetic and the airway, the chest infection risk afterwards, the wound healing and the higher chance of the repair failing.\n\nSay clearly that stopping even for the four weeks before, and the weeks after, makes a measurable difference, and that this is worth doing even if he intends to start again afterwards.\n\nCorrect his assumption directly: tell him whether the operation depends on him stopping, and be honest about it.\n\nOffer the practical help rather than the instruction — the stop smoking service, what it actually provides, nicotine replacement, and how quickly he could be seen.\n\nAgree one thing he is willing to do this week, chosen by him, however small.\n\nExplain what will happen on the day if he has smoked that morning, so that he does not conceal it from the anaesthetist, and make clear that being honest will not cost him his place on the list.\n\nAsk him what he would like recorded in his notes about this conversation, and make sure he leaves with a number he can ring rather than only an intention. Offer to arrange the first contact from here, today, rather than leaving him to make the call himself."
