      "candidateCard": "Thank her for giving you the time at the end of a long school day, and ask how the class as a whole is at the moment.\n\nAsk what has been possible with the recommendations and what has not, in a way that does not require her to justify herself.\n\nListen for the real obstacles — the staffing, the room, the equipment, the other children, and the number of services asking for things.\n\nReport what you have observed about the pupil today, briefly, so that the conversation is about him rather than about compliance.\n\nReduce the list yourself: name the one or two items that would make the biggest difference, and say plainly that the rest can wait.\n\nMake each of those concrete: where the board is kept, when the movement break happens, and who gives it — so that it needs no decision in the moment.\n\nTake on what is not hers: ordering the equipment, writing to the inclusion lead, and speaking to the parents about what happens at home.\n\nAsk what would make this easier for the child in a class of 31, and take her answer seriously — she knows things about him you do not.\n\nAgree what will be in place before your next visit, put a date on it, and offer to come at a time that suits her school day rather than the service's diary."
