      "candidateCard": "Ask her to take you through last night specifically, from the time they went to bed until the morning, rather than asking her how the nights are in general.\n\nAsk how many hours she actually slept last night, and how many nights in the past week have been like that one.\n\nAsk what happens when he tries to leave the house at night, what she does about it, and what she is most frightened of happening.\n\nSay plainly what you can see: that she is exhausted, and that this is not sustainable for either of them.\n\nAddress the belief directly: what asking for help does and does not lead to, and be honest rather than reassuring about it.\n\nExplain what is available that keeps him at home: night sitting, a sitting service, day care, a carer's assessment, and respite that is planned rather than emergency.\n\nDeal with the immediate safety: the door, the lighting, the alarm, and what would happen if he got out.\n\nExplain what usually causes the night-time waking and what can be tried — the routine, the light, the toilet, pain, and the medication review.\n\nAsk what she has given up over the last year — the things that were hers rather than his — and whether anybody has asked her that before.\n\nAgree one thing that changes this week rather than at the next review, and agree who will contact whom."
