      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask her what she wants her first week back at university to look like, in as much detail as she can give you.\n\nEstablish what she can currently do independently: her transfers, dressing, bladder and bowel management, and how long the whole morning routine takes her from waking.\n\nWork through the practical list with her: accommodation, accessible bathroom, the route between buildings, lecture theatres, laboratories, the library, and lifts.\n\nDeal with the timing honestly: which of those take weeks to arrange and cannot be done on arrival.\n\nExplain what disability support services actually do and what they do not, since her fear is that telling them means being managed rather than supported.\n\nAddress the deferral fear directly: what she can ask for, what they can and cannot require, and what her rights are.\n\nAsk what she would find hardest to say to the university herself, and offer either to write it for her or to be present when she says it.\n\nCover the things she may not have thought about: fatigue across a full timetable, pressure care during long lectures, and what she does about a bad day.\n\nAsk who is going with her, who she can call at three in the morning, and what her plan is if the first fortnight goes badly.\n\nAgree a plan with dates working backwards from her start date, and agree exactly what she will do this week."
