      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Start with the bath, since that is what he actually asked for, and assess it properly rather than passing over it to get to what you think matters more.\n\nThen say what you have noticed about the chair and the bedding, and ask about it directly rather than waiting for him to raise it.\n\nAsk how long it has been going on, what made it start, and what actually happens when he tries the stairs now.\n\nExplain what five months of sleeping in a chair does: the legs, the skin, the breathing at night, and what it does to the strength that would get him back upstairs.\n\nLook at his ankles and ask about his breathing lying flat, because this may be heart failure rather than only the knees, and say so.\n\nExplain what could change it: a stair rail, a stairlift, a bed downstairs, or a referral about the knees and the heart — and be honest about the timescales of each.\n\nAsk what he would prefer, and be clear that a bed downstairs is not a defeat and is often temporary.\n\nDeal with the embarrassment: ask whether his daughter and his doctor know, and agree who tells them.\n\nAgree what happens this week, including the referral about his breathing, and arrange to come back yourself rather than leaving it at a single visit."
