      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what has been happening since he came home three days ago: how he is managing the toilet, the armchair, the stairs, and the nights.\n\nAsk him what he does not like about the equipment, and give him room to say more than \"I don't want it\".\n\nListen for what the objects mean to him, and acknowledge it directly when he tells you.\n\nExplain the hip precautions in plain terms and why the height of a seat matters after this operation, using his own hip rather than a general rule.\n\nWatch him stand from his own armchair and get on and off the toilet, so that the conversation rests on what you have both seen.\n\nSeparate the list: which items are about safety in the next six weeks and which are about convenience, and be honest about which is which.\n\nOffer the smallest set that keeps him safe, and offer the versions that look least like hospital equipment.\n\nAgree a date when the temporary items will be collected and taken away, so that nothing in the house feels permanent.\n\nAsk who is around during the week, whether anybody is telephoning him, and what he would do if he needed help in a hurry.\n\nAsk what he would do if he could not get off the toilet at two in the morning, and agree what will be in place for that."
