      "candidateCard": "Ask what worries her most about her mother going home, and let her say the whole of it before you respond to any part of it.\n\nReport what the home visit actually found, item by item, including what your mother could and could not do.\n\nExplain plainly that this is her mother's decision while she has capacity, and that you cannot tell her she cannot go home.\n\nSay that clearly without sounding as though you are dismissing the daughter's concern, and acknowledge that the risk she is worried about is real.\n\nSet out what would be in place: the care package, the equipment, the alarm, the community team, and the review.\n\nBe honest about the risks that remain, and do not pretend the plan removes them.\n\nExplain what would happen if it did not work: that a trial at home is not irreversible, and that a care home would still be there.\n\nAsk what would help her specifically — being telephoned, a copy of the plan, or a way to raise a concern — and arrange it.\n\nAsk whether she has been able to say any of this to her mother directly, and what she thinks would happen if she did.\n\nOffer to have this conversation again with her mother present, and encourage that rather than continuing to discuss her mother in another room."
