      "candidateCard": "Ask what he was told at the hospital, by whom, and exactly when, and take the answer seriously rather than assuming that he misheard it.\n\nAsk whether he has driven, and do not react to the answer in a way that stops him telling you the truth again.\n\nExplain what your assessment found in plain words: what a visual field loss is, and why he does not notice it himself.\n\nDemonstrate it rather than describing it — a simple confrontation test, or asking him to find objects placed to his right — so that he sees the gap himself.\n\nState the position unambiguously: he must not drive, he must inform the licensing authority, and this applies to any vehicle.\n\nExplain what your own obligations are, honestly, so that nothing you do later comes as a surprise to him.\n\nExplain the assessment route that exists: a formal driving assessment, what it involves, and how long it usually takes.\n\nDeal with the income question directly: what happens to his business this month, and where he can get benefits and employment advice.\n\nAsk what else the field loss may be affecting that he has not connected to it — bumping into door frames, missing food on the plate, or crossing roads — and check each one.\n\nAsk what he is going to do tomorrow, and get a clear answer rather than a nod."
