    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Do not defend the system. Say plainly where it is stuck and what you have and have not been able to do, apologise for the part that is yours, and put something in place that helps this week rather than in the spring.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "A client's home, a review visit.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the community occupational therapist.",
      "patientRole": "The 58-year-old wife of a man with motor neurone disease. You recommended a level access shower in March. It is now September, the funding has been agreed but the works have not started, and she telephoned the office in tears last week.",
      "patientConcern": "Her husband is deteriorating faster than the process is moving, and she believes nobody has actually read her file.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask how the two of them have been managing since March, and let her say all of it, without interruption, before you explain anything at all about the process.\n\nApologise for the wait plainly and without conditions, before you list any of the reasons for it.\n\nTell her exactly where the application is: what has been approved, what is outstanding, and who is holding it — with names and dates rather than \"the department\".\n\nBe honest about what you can and cannot influence, and about what you have already done and when.\n\nExplain what you will do this week specifically, and give her a date by which she will hear from you.\n\nDeal with the problem she has today rather than the shower she will have next year: what interim equipment exists, a temporary shower chair, a mobile hoist, or a district nurse to assist with washing.\n\nAsk how she is managing physically, since she has been washing him herself for six months, and ask about her own back and her own sleep.\n\nExplain the deterioration point honestly: that the specification was written for where he will be, and whether it needs revisiting now that six months have passed.\n\nGive her a route to escalate that is not you — the formal complaints process and her local councillor — and say plainly that using either will not affect their care in any way."
    }
