    "profession": "OPTOMETRY",
    "title": "Optometry — A red eye and an old bottle of drops",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Take the history before the reassurance. The borrowed drops are the important part. Refer without alarming him, and be clear about what he must not do tonight.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Optometry practice, a walk-in appointment at the end of the afternoon.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the optometrist.",
      "patientRole": "A 44-year-old man whose right eye has been red and painful for three days, worse in the light. He has been putting in drops left over from his wife's prescription last year, and the eye is now worse.",
      "patientConcern": "He wants \"something stronger\" so that he can get through a busy week, and he does not want to be sent anywhere.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what the pain is like, whether light hurts, whether the sight in that eye has changed, and whether there is any discharge.\n\nAsk exactly what the borrowed drops were, how often he has used them, and for how many days.\n\nAsk whether he wears contact lenses, whether anything went into the eye at work, and whether he has had a cold sore recently.\n\nAsk when he last had his eyes examined, and whether either eye has ever done anything like this before.\n\nExamine the eye in front of him and say what you are looking at and why.\n\nMeasure the sight in that eye today, tell him the result, and compare it with the other eye.\n\nExplain plainly why borrowing somebody else's drops is dangerous: some kinds make certain infections very much worse, and the eye cannot tell him which one he has.\n\nExplain what you have found and what it could be, without naming the worst possibility as though it were settled.\n\nTell him he needs to be seen at the eye unit today, and explain in one sentence why this cannot wait until the weekend.\n\nTell him to stop the borrowed drops now, and that he should not put anything else in the eye until he has been seen.\n\nDeal with the busy week honestly rather than dismissing it, and be clear about what an untreated infection of this kind can cost him.\n\nTelephone the referral while he is with you, and tell him where to go and what to take."
    }
