    "profession": "OPTOMETRY",
    "title": "Optometry — Telling a patient she has glaucoma",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Give the diagnosis plainly and early, then deal with the mother before anything else — because until that is addressed she will not hear a word about treatment. Be honest about what cannot be recovered and clear about what treatment does.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Optometry practice, at a follow-up appointment after repeat testing.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the optometrist.",
      "patientRole": "A 62-year-old woman whose repeated pressures, discs and fields are consistent with chronic open angle glaucoma. Her mother was registered blind from glaucoma.",
      "patientConcern": "She watched her mother lose her sight over fifteen years and believes she is now watching the same thing begin.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Give the diagnosis in one plain sentence, early, and then stop and let her respond before adding anything.\n\nAsk about her mother: what happened, over what period, and what she remembers of it.\n\nExplain honestly what is different now — that hers has been found by testing before she noticed anything, and that her mother's generation was usually diagnosed after the damage was done.\n\nExplain what glaucoma actually is, in ordinary words: the nerve rather than the surface of the eye, and why it is painless.\n\nBe honest that the field loss already present will not come back, and say it plainly rather than letting her discover it later.\n\nExplain what treatment does: it lowers the pressure to protect what remains, it usually works, and most people treated from this stage keep useful sight for life.\n\nExplain what happens next in practical terms: the hospital referral, roughly when she will be seen, what they will do at that appointment, and that drops are the usual first treatment.\n\nExplain what this means for her driving today, since she will be wondering and may not ask.\n\nExplain what her part is — drops every day, appointments kept — and be honest that this is the part that decides the outcome.\n\nAnswer the question she is most likely to ask — whether she will go blind — honestly, without promising and without frightening her.\n\nTell her that her children should be tested from the age of forty and free of charge, and agree what she will say to them."
    }
