      "candidateCard": "Ask the child himself first, at his level and before his mother answers, what he thinks of the glasses and whether anything about them is uncomfortable.\n\nCheck the fit in front of them: whether they slip, whether they mark his nose, whether they sit straight, and whether he is looking over the top of them.\n\nAsk the mother what the mornings are actually like, and take the exhaustion seriously.\n\nAsk about school specifically — what has been said, by whom, and whether the teacher knows.\n\nExplain to both of them why the glasses matter more for him than for most children: that the right eye is behind and that the glasses are the treatment rather than a help.\n\nBe honest about the window: that this treatment works best before about seven, and say it without turning it into a threat.\n\nDeal with the frame itself: offer a different style chosen by him, a sports band, and a spare pair, and let him choose today.\n\nOffer to write to the school about the name-calling and about the seating.\n\nAsk whether anybody at home wears glasses, since a child who is the only one in the house often wears them better once that changes.\n\nAgree what will be tried for the next month, ask the child himself what he is willing to do, and book the review before they leave rather than asking them to telephone."
