      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what happened last time he had drops, and let him tell the whole story.\n\nAcknowledge plainly that he should have been warned and that what happened to him was avoidable.\n\nExplain what you can and cannot see through an undilated pupil, and be specific about which part of the retina is hidden.\n\nExplain why this matters more for him than for most patients, using his diabetes and the six years rather than a general rule.\n\nExplain what you would be looking for and what finding it early would change.\n\nExplain, without alarming him, what new vessel growth and swelling at the macula are, and that both can be well advanced before a person notices any difference in their sight.\n\nAsk how long he has had the diagnosis and what his last blood sugar reading was, since both change how urgently this examination needs doing.\n\nSolve the practical problem: how long the drops last, the timing of the appointment, sunglasses, and arranging it for a day he is not working or not driving.\n\nOffer the alternatives honestly: retinal photography without dilation, what it does and does not show, and whether it would be enough in his case.\n\nDeal with the missed screening appointments, without a lecture, and find out whether the obstacle was the appointment times, the travel, or something nobody has explained to him properly, then offer to help him get another one.\n\nAgree what will happen: either drops today with a plan for getting home, or a booked appointment on a non-working day, and put the date in before he leaves."
