      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask exactly what she is experiencing, when, and doing what — rather than asking whether they are better.\n\nAsk whether the swimming is worse in a particular light, at a particular distance, or when she turns her head, since each answer points somewhere different.\n\nAsk what she wore before these and how she used them, because years of separate reading glasses change what her eyes now expect.\n\nRecheck in front of her: the prescription, the lens centres, the fit, the frame position and the height of the reading portion.\n\nSay out loud what you are checking and what you find, so that she can see this is being taken seriously rather than defended.\n\nExplain what a varifocal actually does and why the peripheral distortion exists, in ordinary words.\n\nTake the stairs seriously and give the specific advice: point the nose at what she wants to see, look through the top of the lens on stairs, and hold the rail while she is adapting.\n\nBe honest about the adaptation period, how long it usually takes, and that most people adapt but not everybody does.\n\nSay clearly what you will do if she does not adapt: a different lens design, a wider corridor, or separate pairs — and what that would cost her, which is the question underneath.\n\nAddress the refund question directly rather than avoiding it, and explain the practice's position plainly.\n\nAgree what she will do for the next two weeks, and book a review at which the decision will be made rather than left open."
