      "candidateCard": "Ask when the floaters and the flashes began, whether the shadow has grown since yesterday, and how much of her side vision it now covers.\n\nAsk about any recent blow to the head or the eye, any previous eye surgery, and how short-sighted she has been since childhood.\n\nExplain what you have found in ordinary words: a tear in the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye, and what that layer does.\n\nExplain what happens if a tear is left, and why the next few days matter, without using the word emergency as a threat.\n\nExplain what the hospital is likely to do, that it is usually done with the eye numbed rather than asleep, and that she would expect to go home the same day.\n\nTell her plainly that you are referring her today, and telephone the eye unit while she is still in the room rather than after she leaves.\n\nDeal with the flight honestly: the surgeon must answer it, some treatments make flying unsafe for a period, and it is not a decision you can make for her today.\n\nTell her what to do while she waits: no heavy lifting or strenuous exercise, and go straight to the emergency eye service if the shadow spreads.\n\nCheck that someone can take her, since she should not drive while that shadow is there.\n\nAsk her to tell you back what she is going to do today and where she is going."
