      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what she has actually noticed, and separate small print becoming harder from anything bent, blurred or missing in the middle.\n\nAsk what happened to her sister and what she was told at the time.\n\nExplain what the photographs show in plain words, and what the macula is and what it is for.\n\nExplain the difference between the slow form she has and the sudden form, and what each one does over what length of time.\n\nCorrect the fear directly: this condition does not take away the side vision she walks about her flat with.\n\nExplain what genuinely helps — not smoking, blood pressure, sunlight protection, green vegetables and oily fish — and be honest about how much each one is worth.\n\nExplain the eye vitamins honestly: who they are for, who they do nothing for, and that they are not a cure.\n\nGive her the Amsler chart, and have her use it in front of you: reading glasses on, one eye covered, looking at the central dot.\n\nBe specific about the warning signs — straight lines bending, a new blur or a missing patch in the middle of her sight — and that these mean an urgent appointment, not a wait.\n\nTalk about the practical side as well: better light for reading, a magnifier, and what the low-vision service can do.\n\nAgree when she will be seen again, and write the Amsler instructions down for her before she leaves."
