      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what he watched, what was said in it, and which part worried him most, before you offer any counter-argument.\n\nAsk about the leg aches in detail: where, when they started against the tablet, whether they are symmetrical, and whether they are worse on exertion.\n\nExplain how a genuine muscle side effect is distinguished from ordinary aches, what test would settle it, and that it is reversible.\n\nExplain honestly what the evidence shows about memory and this class of medicine, including where it is uncertain, without dismissing his concern as nonsense.\n\nExplain what this medicine is doing in his particular case after a heart attack, rather than in general, and what the six weeks off may already have changed.\n\nAddress the wider complaint — that nobody has explained his five medicines — and offer to go through each one with him now, saying what it is for.\n\nOffer the practical alternatives that exist if this one does not suit him: a lower dose, a different agent, alternate days, or a supervised trial off and on.\n\nArrange a blood test and a review with the prescriber rather than leaving the decision here, and explain what the test would show.\n\nAsk what would make him comfortable restarting, if anything, and write his questions down so that he does not have to argue for them in a short appointment.\n\nAgree what he will do in the meantime and make clear that the decision is his, while being honest about what you would advise."
