    "title": "Pharmacy — An early request for a controlled drug",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Do not accuse and do not simply refuse. Establish the facts calmly, explain what you can and cannot do and why, and take real steps to get him seen — including contacting the out-of-hours service yourself rather than leaving him to it.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Community pharmacy, Friday evening, twenty minutes before closing.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the pharmacist. His prescription is not due for six days.",
      "patientRole": "A 39-year-old man collecting modified-release morphine for chronic back pain. He says his tablets were \"taken by mistake\" by a relative and that he will be in agony over the weekend.",
      "patientConcern": "He is in real pain and is also frightened of being treated as an addict; the story he has given may or may not be complete.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask him to tell you what has happened, without interrupting, and take the account seriously.\n\nEstablish the facts you need: when the last supply was collected, how many he has taken, how many should remain, and when the missing tablets were last seen.\n\nAsk directly about his pain now — where, how severe, whether it is his usual pain or something different — and about any symptoms of withdrawal.\n\nExplain plainly what you cannot do and why: the legal position on an early supply, and the fact that this is not a judgement about him.\n\nExplain what you can do: contact the prescriber, contact the out-of-hours service, and record what has happened so that whoever sees him has the full picture.\n\nDo that contacting in front of him rather than telling him to do it himself, and tell him what you are going to say.\n\nAsk whether the tablets could be at risk in the house, and give safe storage advice without suggesting who took them.\n\nExplain what he can safely take in the meantime from what he already has, and what he must not add on top.\n\nAsk how his pain has been managed generally over the last few months, since a request like this is often a sign that the underlying plan is not working.\n\nAgree what will happen if the out-of-hours service cannot see him tonight, and make sure he leaves with a specific next step and a time rather than a general suggestion."
    }
