    "subTest": "SPEAKING",
    "taskType": "SPEAKING_ROLEPLAY",
    "profession": "PHARMACY",
    "title": "Pharmacy — A pharmacy dispensing error",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "This is a candour conversation. Say what happened before she has to ask, apologise without conditions, explain the actual effect, arrange the medical review yourself, and tell her about the investigation and how to complain.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Community pharmacy consultation room, the morning after the error was discovered.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the responsible pharmacist. Your team dispensed the wrong strength.",
      "patientRole": "A 61-year-old woman who has taken 10 mg of a blood pressure tablet instead of 2.5 mg for eleven days, because the wrong strength was dispensed. She has felt dizzy and has fallen once, without injury.",
      "patientConcern": "She trusted the pharmacy completely and now wonders what else may have been wrong over the years.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Tell her at the start exactly what happened: which medicine, which strength, for how long, and that it was the pharmacy's error.\n\nApologise clearly and without adding an explanation of how it happened before the apology.\n\nAsk how she has been, and let her describe the dizziness and the fall fully before you interpret it.\n\nExplain what the higher dose does and why it caused what she felt, and what you would expect now that it has stopped.\n\nExplain what has already been done: the correct strength supplied, the prescriber informed, and any monitoring that is needed.\n\nArrange the medical review yourself, in front of her, and tell her when it is and what it is for.\n\nExplain the incident investigation: that it will happen, what it looks at, and that its purpose is to stop it happening to somebody else.\n\nTell her she can have a written account and how to make a formal complaint, and give her the details without waiting to be asked.\n\nAsk whether she would like a member of her family to be told, and whether she would prefer to speak to somebody other than you in future.\n\nAnswer honestly if she asks whether this has happened before, and address her wider worry about her other medicines with a specific offer — a full check of her current supplies with her, today, if she would like it."
    }
