    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Do not lecture her about confidentiality. Explain it once, plainly, and then spend the conversation on what you can do — which is a great deal — rather than on what you cannot.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Community pharmacy, at the counter, then moved to the consultation room.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the pharmacist. The mother is a regular customer who collects her own prescriptions and has never given consent for anyone else to be told about them.",
      "patientRole": "The 52-year-old daughter of an 80-year-old customer. She wants to know what her mother is taking, believes she is not taking it properly, and has asked you to change the supply to a weekly box without telling her mother.",
      "patientConcern": "Her worry is genuine — she has found tablets on the floor and old boxes in a drawer — and she does not understand why you cannot simply tell her.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Move the conversation out of the shop and into the consultation room before anything else.\n\nListen to what she has actually seen at her mother's house, and take it seriously as evidence rather than as an opinion.\n\nExplain, once and in plain words, why you cannot discuss her mother's medicines without her mother's agreement, and make clear that this protects her mother rather than the pharmacy.\n\nSay clearly what you can do without any consent at all: general advice, information about services, and how to raise a concern about someone's safety.\n\nExplain how consent could be obtained easily — her mother coming in, telephoning, or agreeing in writing — and offer to raise it with her mother yourself at the next collection.\n\nExplain the weekly box: what it is, what it does and does not solve, that it cannot be started without her mother's knowledge, and what would be involved.\n\nAsk what worries her most about her mother's safety, and if the answer suggests real risk, explain what you would do about it and who you would contact.\n\nOffer a medicines review at home, with her mother's agreement, and explain what that would involve.\n\nAgree what you will each do before her mother's next prescription is due, and give her a way to contact you."
    }
