    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Answer the heartburn question properly, but do not let the headache, the swelling and the timing pass. Ask the specific questions that matter at 29 weeks, and be clear about what needs assessment today rather than advice.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Community pharmacy consultation room.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the pharmacist.",
      "patientRole": "A 31-year-old woman who is 29 weeks pregnant, asking for the same heartburn remedy she used before she was pregnant. She has also mentioned, in passing, that her ankles have been swollen and she has had a headache for two days.",
      "patientConcern": "She came in about heartburn and is not worried about the headache, which she attributes to the heat.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask about the heartburn: how long, what brings it on, what she has tried, and whether it wakes her at night.\n\nAsk what she has already taken during the pregnancy, including anything bought or borrowed.\n\nAsk about the headache directly: how long, where, whether anything relieves it, and whether she has had any visual disturbance, flashing lights, pain below the ribs on the right, nausea or vomiting.\n\nAsk about the swelling: how quickly it came on, whether it involves her hands or face, and whether her rings still fit.\n\nAsk when she was last seen for antenatal care, what her blood pressure has been, and whether protein has ever been found in her urine.\n\nExplain plainly why you are asking these questions and what combination you are trying to exclude, without frightening her more than is warranted.\n\nSay clearly that she needs to be assessed today rather than tomorrow, tell her where to go — maternity triage rather than the general practice — and give her the number.\n\nContact the maternity unit yourself while she is with you, and tell her what you have said.\n\nDeal with the heartburn as well: what is safe at 29 weeks, what she can do about position, meals and timing, and what you are supplying today.\n\nCheck that she has someone to go with her and that she is not planning to drive herself, and ask her to tell you back where she is going and why."
    }
