      "candidateCard": "Ask why he wants to stop, and separate the \"I feel fine\" from the practical difficulty of getting here.\n\nAcknowledge honestly how well he has done, and be specific about what has improved.\n\nShow him what he cannot feel: ask him to walk while you point out what happens to his hip, or record it and let him watch.\n\nTest the abductor strength in front of him and tell him the number, and compare it with the other side.\n\nExplain what a weak abductor does over the next year: the limp becoming permanent, the load on the other hip and the knee, and the effect on walking distance.\n\nExplain that the pain going is not the same as the recovery finishing, and why that is the commonest reason people stop too early.\n\nDeal with the two buses: offer a home programme with video, a telephone review, a local gym referral, or fewer but longer appointments.\n\nAgree the specific target that would let him be discharged — a strength grade and a walking test — so that discharge is a measurement rather than an opinion.\n\nAsk what he wants to be able to do by the summer, and set the programme against that rather than against an appointment schedule.\n\nAgree the plan and the review date, and be clear that stopping is his decision and that he can come back at any point."
