      "patientConcern": "The marathon is a charity run in memory of his father and he has raised a significant sum; stopping is not a neutral option for him.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what his week actually looks like — the distance, the surface, the pace, the hills and the shoes — rather than asking whether he has rested.\n\nThank him for telling you the truth about the mileage, and make clear it changes the plan rather than your opinion of him.\n\nAsk what the marathon means to him and why this one, and listen to the whole answer.\n\nExplain what a tendon does under load: why it needs load to heal, why the amount matters, and why six weeks of unchanged training has produced six weeks of unchanged symptoms.\n\nBe honest about what is likely if nothing changes, including the possibility that he does not make the start line at all.\n\nNegotiate rather than prohibit: propose a specific modified plan with numbers — reduced distance, altered surface, days between runs, and cross-training that protects his fitness.\n\nExplain the loading programme, how often, how heavy, and how long before he should expect a change.\n\nAgree the markers you will both use to decide, and what would make you say the marathon is not possible.\n\nAsk about the pain pattern that tells you both how it is going: the first steps in the morning, the warm-up, and the day after a run.\n\nAgree a review date before the entry deadline, and be clear that the decision will be made on the findings rather than on the calendar."
