      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask how it has been going, in a way that makes an honest answer possible rather than in a way that invites the answer you are hoping for.\n\nWhen he tells you, do not react as though it is a failure; thank him for saying so rather than telling you what he thought you wanted to hear.\n\nAsk exactly what he tried, when, and what stopped it — the time, the equipment, the pain, the tiredness or the privacy.\n\nAsk him to describe a typical twenty-four hours, including his shifts and his sleep, so that the programme is built around a real day.\n\nExplain honestly what the exercises are for and what will not happen without them, without turning it into a warning.\n\nRebuild the programme with him: fewer exercises, no equipment, two or three minutes at a time, attached to something he already does every day.\n\nAgree the minimum that is worth doing, and be honest that a small amount done daily beats a long programme done never.\n\nAsk what would make it more likely: a video on his telephone, a reminder, a chart, or doing it at work.\n\nAsk whether anything at work is making the shoulder worse — the wheel, the loading, the cab position — and what could be changed about it.\n\nAgree what he will actually do before the next appointment, in his words, and check it against his shift pattern before he leaves. Agree what you will do differently if this programme also does not happen."
