      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Say early and plainly that you believe her pain is real, and do not make her earn it first.\n\nAsk her to tell you what has happened over the four years, and let it run rather than steering it.\n\nAsk what she has been told, by whom, and which of those conversations was the worst.\n\nExplain, without jargon, how a nervous system can become sensitised, and why a normal scan and real pain are not a contradiction.\n\nBe honest about what is known and what is not, including where the science is uncertain.\n\nAsk what the pain has actually taken from her — work, sleep, exercise, people — and which of those she wants back most.\n\nExplain what this service does and does not offer, so that her expectations and yours are the same before you start.\n\nAgree a first goal that is small, specific and hers, rather than a reduction in the pain score.\n\nAsk about her sleep and her mood without implying that either is the cause, and explain why both are part of the treatment rather than an alternative explanation for the pain.\n\nExplain what the folder she has brought is useful for and what it is not, and offer to read it properly rather than glancing at it.\n\nAgree how you will work together, what she should do if she has a bad week, and make clear that a flare-up will not be treated as a failure or as evidence that she was exaggerating."
