      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what happened to his brother, and let him tell it without hurrying him to the point.\n\nAsk when the night pain started, how many nights a week it wakes him, and where he has been sleeping.\n\nAsk whether anyone else at home knows how bad the foot has become.\n\nAsk what he thinks will happen to him if he goes, and answer that specific fear rather than a general one.\n\nShow him the toe and the findings, and explain what the night pain is telling you both.\n\nExplain what is actually being asked of him: an assessment and a scan, not an operation and not an admission.\n\nBe honest about what happens if nothing is done, without threatening him with the worst outcome.\n\nBe honest, too, about what the assessment cannot promise, so that he is not being sold certainty.\n\nAsk what would make it possible: someone with him, a morning appointment, transport, or knowing in advance who he will see.\n\nExplore whether anything at home is stopping him — a pet, a wife who needs him, or the cost of parking.\n\nOffer to write down the questions he wants answered and to send them ahead with the referral.\n\nBe clear about what would mean going to the emergency department that day, whatever he decides about the referral.\n\nAsk him what he wants to do, and if he still refuses, agree a short review and record his decision without making it the end of the conversation."
