      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask him to tell you what he noticed, when, and what he did about it.\n\nAsk directly how he is feeling about it, and let the answer stand rather than correcting it.\n\nTell him plainly that finding it at this size, and coming in the same week, is the reason this will be a short episode rather than a long one.\n\nExplain honestly why ulcers recur at the same site: the shape of the foot and the loading have not changed, and the scar is weaker than the skin around it.\n\nGo through what actually changed in the last few months — footwear, weight, activity, a new job, or a worn-out insole — and look for the specific cause rather than blaming him.\n\nExamine the insoles and the shoes in front of him and say what you find.\n\nExplain what the treatment will be this time and give him a realistic timescale based on the size.\n\nSet out what will be different from last time, so that it is not simply a repetition.\n\nAnswer the \"no point\" directly, with what the last year actually achieved and what it prevented.\n\nAsk whether the year of treatment cost him anything he has not mentioned — work, sleep, or his mood — and offer to raise it with his general practitioner.\n\nAgree the offloading and the review date today, and tell him what he should look for at home."
