      "candidateCard": "Ask how long he has been going barefoot indoors, and where in the house and garden he does it.\n\nShow him the splinter you removed and the wound it left, and ask him when he thinks it went in.\n\nTest his sensation in front of him with the monofilament and let him watch you touch the foot he cannot feel.\n\nExplain what the test result means in ordinary words: that pain is the alarm that tells a person to stop, and that his alarm has been disconnected.\n\nExplain what actually happens next — that a wound he cannot feel is walked on all day, and that this is how ulcers begin — without predicting amputation to frighten him.\n\nAsk what stops him wearing something indoors: the heat, the habit, or the feel of shoes in the house.\n\nOffer real alternatives rather than a prohibition: indoor shoes kept by the door, a pair for the garden only, and seamless socks in the evening.\n\nDeal with the garden specifically, since that is where the splinter came from, and agree what he will wear out there.\n\nTeach him the daily check: both feet, between the toes, and both heels with a mirror or with his wife's help, at the same time each day.\n\nGive him the specific things that mean telephoning the same day rather than waiting for his next appointment.\n\nAgree one change he is willing to make today, and write it down for him."
