      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what her worry about the cast actually is, and let her set out the whole of it before you answer.\n\nAsk what she does for her mother in a normal day, and which of those things she thinks the cast would stop.\n\nShow her the wound measurements over the five weeks and let her see for herself that nothing has changed.\n\nExplain in plain words why the wound is not healing: that the pressure that made it is still being applied to it every time she stands.\n\nExplain what a cast does that a dressing cannot, and why offloading is the treatment rather than an addition to it.\n\nBe honest about what the cast will mean: how it is worn, that it is not removable, how she washes, and how long it is likely to be needed.\n\nDeal with the driving directly, since that is her real question, and be clear about what she must check with her insurer.\n\nExplore the alternatives honestly — a removable walker, felted offloading — and be clear that they work less well and why.\n\nAsk what support exists for her mother, and offer to write to her general practitioner or to social services about a short period of extra care.\n\nBe honest about what happens if the ulcer does not heal, without using amputation as a threat.\n\nAgree a decision point: either a cast today, or a walker for two weeks with a measured review, and book that review before she leaves."
