      "candidateCard": "Ask what her training week actually looks like now: distance, surfaces, hills, speed work and shoes.\n\nAsk what changed in the month before the pain began, since something usually did.\n\nAsk what she has already tried herself, and whether anything helped even briefly.\n\nAsk what the pain does across a run — at the start, during, afterwards, and the next morning.\n\nAsk what the marathon means to her, and listen to the answer properly before you say anything about her tendon.\n\nExplain what you have found in the tendon and what the changes mean, in ordinary words.\n\nBe honest about what continuing at this load is likely to do, and be equally honest about the uncertainty rather than predicting rupture.\n\nExplain what the treatment actually is: a progressive loading programme, and why complete rest is not the answer either.\n\nNegotiate the training rather than banning it: what could change in surface, hills, speed, and weekly distance, and what must stay.\n\nBe clear about the specific signs that would mean stopping altogether, and agree them with her now while she is calm.\n\nDiscuss the marathon itself honestly: what running it might cost her, and what a slower finish or a different event would mean.\n\nSet out the referrals and what each would add, and be clear about what will not help.\n\nAgree a written plan and a review in three weeks, at which the decision about the marathon is made on evidence rather than on the day."
