      "candidateRole": "You are the podiatrist.",
      "patientRole": "A 59-year-old man whose walking distance has fallen from half a mile to about eighty metres in a year. His ankle brachial pressure index is 0.5 on the right. He has smoked thirty a day since he was sixteen and has told two clinicians already that he is not interested in stopping.",
      "patientConcern": "He has been lectured about smoking at every appointment for a decade and has stopped listening to it.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what he can and cannot do now, and what he has given up in the last year.\n\nAsk what has been said to him about smoking before, and what he thought of it.\n\nShow him his own measurements and explain what the number on the right actually means.\n\nExplain what is happening in the arteries of his leg in plain words, and how it connects to the pain on walking.\n\nBe accurate rather than dramatic: what stopping smoking does for walking distance, what it does for the risk of losing the leg, and what it will not undo.\n\nSay once, clearly, that this is the single most useful thing he could do, and then leave it rather than repeating it.\n\nAsk what he would want if he ever did decide, and tell him what is available without pressing him to take it today.\n\nGive him the other half of the treatment properly: supervised exercise, what walking to the point of pain and beyond actually does, and how often.\n\nExplain what his medication is for, and check he is taking it.\n\nBe clear about the warning signs — pain at rest, pain at night, a wound that does not heal, a change of colour — and what each of them means he should do.\n\nAgree what he will actually try before the next appointment, and let him choose which part."
