      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what she has read and what specifically worries her about it.\n\nAsk whether anyone has ever explained the reason for each scan to her, and what she was told.\n\nTake the question seriously out loud, and say that it is a sensible thing to ask rather than an awkward one.\n\nExplain in ordinary words what the dose of a scan like hers is, and compare it with something she can picture, such as background radiation over a period of time.\n\nBe honest about the risk rather than dismissing it: say what the added lifetime risk is thought to be, and set it against the everyday risk of the same illness.\n\nExplain that every request is justified by someone before it is booked, and what that actually involves rather than describing it as a formality.\n\nExplain what the department does to keep the dose as low as possible: the protocol, the scanned length, and the settings used for someone her age.\n\nExplain what the scans are for in her case: what they are looking for, and what finding it early would change.\n\nTell her honestly that whether she still needs three-monthly scans is a question for her oncologist and not for you.\n\nOffer to write her question into her record so that it is raised at her next appointment rather than forgotten.\n\nAsk whether she still wants to go ahead today, and accept whichever answer she gives."
