      "candidateCard": "Introduce yourself to him first, by name, and ask him how he likes to be spoken to.\n\nAsk him what he has been told about today, and whether anyone explained it to him at home.\n\nAsk the support worker what he understands, how he communicates, and what usually helps him.\n\nAsk what happened at the hospital where he refused, and what was different that day.\n\nAsk whether he has a hospital passport or a care plan, and read it rather than asking the questions again.\n\nDeal with the noise and the lights first, since they are distressing him now, and see what can be turned down or switched off.\n\nExplain the X-ray to him in short sentences, one thing at a time, using words he uses.\n\nShow him the equipment before anything is asked of him, and let him touch the plate if he wants to.\n\nExplain what he will have to do, and how long \"still\" means, in seconds he can count.\n\nOffer him choices where they exist: standing or sitting, which door, who stays with him, and whether he goes first or watches someone else.\n\nAgree a way for him to say stop, and show that it works by stopping when he uses it.\n\nAgree with him and the support worker what will happen if he says no today, and make sure that is not treated as a failure."
