      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask her what part of it frightens her: the enclosed space, the noise, being alone, or not being able to get out.\n\nAsk whether anything like this has happened to her before, and what helped or did not help then.\n\nSay plainly that a great many people find this difficult and that stopping is not failing, before you discuss anything practical.\n\nDescribe honestly what the scan involves: the size of the opening, how far in she goes, how long each sequence lasts, and how long the whole appointment takes.\n\nExplain the things that are in her control: the alarm bulb in her hand, the intercom between every sequence, and that the scan stops the moment she asks.\n\nOffer the adjustments that exist: the mirror, the cold air, music, an eye mask, someone she trusts holding her ankle, and going in feet first where the examination allows.\n\nBe honest that some of these will not apply to a head scan, rather than promising her comfort you cannot deliver.\n\nOffer a graded approach: sitting on the table, then lying, then going part way in, with no obligation to continue.\n\nSet out the alternatives properly: coming back with medication from her doctor, the wider scanner at another site, and scanning under anaesthetic, and be honest about what each involves.\n\nAnswer her real fear directly: explain what happens next if today does not work, so that she leaves knowing she has not lost her place.\n\nAsk her what she wants to do now, and accept the answer without argument."
