      "candidateCard": "Ask what he has read and where, and ask him to tell you what worries him most about it.\n\nAsk whether anyone has explained the scan to him already, and what he understood from it.\n\nAsk about his brother, since that is where the fear actually comes from, and listen properly.\n\nExplain what the contrast is and what it does in the pictures, in ordinary words.\n\nShow him his own blood result and explain what that number means about how his kidneys are working.\n\nBe honest rather than reassuring: explain that a small risk exists, who it is highest in, and why he is not in that group.\n\nExplain what would happen without the contrast: what the scan could and could not answer, and why the doctor asked for it this way.\n\nAsk about the things that genuinely matter here: his fluid intake today, any water tablets, any anti-inflammatory tablets, and whether he takes metformin.\n\nExplain what the injection feels like — the warm flush, the taste, the feeling of having wet himself — so that nothing during the scan alarms him.\n\nExplain what the department does to reduce the risk: the dose used, the hydration advice, and the checking of his bloods beforehand.\n\nBe clear that the decision is his, and that refusing does not mean being sent away without help.\n\nAsk him what he wants to do, and if he agrees, tell him what happens for the rest of the day."
