    "title": "Radiography — A man who thinks he will be radioactive",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Correct the belief clearly and early, then check what else he has assumed. Distinguish external beam treatment from the treatments where precautions genuinely apply.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Radiotherapy department, at the pre-treatment appointment.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the therapeutic radiographer.",
      "patientRole": "A 70-year-old man due to begin external beam radiotherapy for prostate cancer. He has told his daughter that he cannot see his grandchildren for the seven weeks of treatment, and he has already cancelled a holiday with them.",
      "patientConcern": "He believes he will carry radiation home with him and that it will harm the children, and nobody has corrected this because nobody knew he thought it.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what he has been told so far and where the idea came from.\n\nAsk what he has already changed or cancelled because of it, before you correct anything.\n\nSay plainly and early that external beam radiotherapy does not make him radioactive and that his grandchildren are not at risk from him.\n\nExplain how the treatment actually works: that the beam is switched on and off, and that nothing remains in his body afterwards.\n\nExplain, so that he understands why he has heard otherwise, that some treatments do involve a source placed inside the body or an injection, and that his is not one of them.\n\nCheck what else he has assumed: whether he thinks he must sleep apart, use separate crockery, or avoid pregnant relatives, and correct each one specifically.\n\nExplain what the treatment will actually be like day to day: the time in the room, the number of visits, and how long each appointment takes.\n\nBe honest about the side effects he should expect, so that reassurance about one thing does not sound like reassurance about everything.\n\nExplain the fatigue in particular, when it usually begins, and how it may affect what he can do with the children.\n\nEncourage him to reinstate what he has cancelled, and offer to explain this to his daughter directly if that would help.\n\nAgree what he will tell his family, and give him the department's number for the questions that will occur to him tonight."
    }
