    "title": "Radiography — A woman who wants to stop treatment",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Take the complaint seriously rather than defending the department. Deal with the pain first, be honest about what the remaining fractions will and will not add, and do not pressure her.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Radiotherapy department, at an on-treatment review before fraction 12 of 15.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the therapeutic radiographer.",
      "patientRole": "A 61-year-old woman having radiotherapy after breast surgery. She has moist desquamation in the inframammary fold, pain of 7 out of 10, and has said she is not coming back after today.",
      "patientConcern": "She was told the skin reaction would be \"like sunburn\", she feels she was not warned, and she has lost confidence in what she is told.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask her to tell you what has been happening at home, and how she has been managing the last week.\n\nAsk what she was told at the start and by whom, and acknowledge without excuses that \"like sunburn\" did not prepare her for this.\n\nLook at the skin with her and describe what you see, so that she knows it is being taken seriously.\n\nAsk specifically about the pain: when it is worst, what she is taking, and whether anything helps.\n\nExplain honestly that the reaction usually peaks a week to ten days after the last treatment, so that stopping now would not spare her the worst of it.\n\nExplain what the remaining three fractions contribute, in terms of the benefit she was treated for, without overstating it.\n\nBe clear that stopping is her decision and that she will not be treated differently for making it.\n\nSet out what can be done today: better dressings, stronger analgesia through the review doctor, and a change to how the fold is managed.\n\nOffer the practical adjustments: a later appointment time, help getting the dressing on at home, and a district nurse referral.\n\nAsk what would need to change for her to be willing to come tomorrow, and answer that specific thing.\n\nAgree either a plan to finish or a review with the doctor today, and make sure her skin is dressed before she leaves whichever she chooses."
    }
