    "title": "Radiography — Explaining the mask",
    "prompt": "Read your role-play card. You have three minutes to prepare, then carry out the consultation. Speak your part aloud.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "roleplay",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 0,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "Describe the mask honestly, including the parts that are difficult. Do not minimise it. Offer the things that genuinely help and let him see the room before he decides.",
    "payload": {
      "setting": "Radiotherapy pre-treatment clinic, before the planning appointment.",
      "candidateRole": "You are the therapeutic radiographer.",
      "patientRole": "A 58-year-old man due to have radiotherapy to the neck. He has been told he will wear a fitted mask clipped to the table and has become extremely anxious. He has not slept for two nights and is considering refusing treatment.",
      "patientConcern": "He is frightened of being fixed down and unable to move or speak, and he has not told his family that this is why he may refuse.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what he has been told about the mask and what picture he has in his head.\n\nAsk whether he has ever had trouble in an enclosed space before, and what happened then.\n\nAsk what specifically frightens him: the fixing, the covering of his face, the not speaking, or the time.\n\nDescribe the mask honestly: what it is made of, that it is warm and soft when moulded, and how it firms as it cools.\n\nDescribe exactly how it is made, step by step, and how long each stage takes.\n\nBe honest about what it feels like when it is clipped down, rather than telling him it will be fine.\n\nExplain why it is needed: that the treatment must land within a very small margin, and what movement would cost him.\n\nExplain the openings for the eyes, nose and mouth, and be clear about what he can and cannot do while wearing it.\n\nExplain how he tells staff to stop: the hand signal, the camera, the intercom, and that staff are watching him for every second.\n\nOffer what helps: seeing the room and the mask beforehand, a practice run, music, a dry run without treatment, and medication from his doctor if he wants it.\n\nAsk whether his family know how frightened he is, and offer to help him tell them.\n\nAgree the next step — a visit to the treatment room today — and book it before he leaves."
    }
