      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask him about the chain and let him tell you what it means before you ask him for anything.\n\nBe clear early that this is a safety matter and not a matter of departmental preference.\n\nExplain what the scanner actually is: a magnet that is always on, and one that cannot be switched off for a moment.\n\nExplain in plain terms what happens to metal near it: the pull, the speed, and what that would do to him at the neck.\n\nExplain the second risk, that metal can heat during the scan, since he may not have heard of that one.\n\nExplain that the chain would also spoil the pictures of his lower back if it were anywhere near, and that the scan might have to be repeated.\n\nAsk what he is actually worried about, and check whether it is losing it rather than removing it.\n\nSolve that problem specifically: a signed valuables envelope, the locker he keeps the key to, or giving it to the person who came with him.\n\nOffer the alternative he may not have thought of: taping it to his ankle if it can be removed and kept on his person, if the examination allows it.\n\nBe honest that if it cannot come off at all, the scan cannot go ahead today, and say what would happen then rather than leaving it as a threat.\n\nGive him time and privacy to decide, and check whether he wants someone with him while he takes it off."
