      "candidateCard": "Ask what she has read and where, and what made her think of the tongue.\n\nAsk when he started talking, how much he says now, and whether he understands everything said to him.\n\nAsk whether his hearing has ever been tested, and whether he had many ear infections as a baby.\n\nAsk what strangers do not understand, and get examples of actual words rather than a general description.\n\nExamine his tongue with her watching, and show her what he can do with it.\n\nExplain what a tongue-tie restricts, and which speech sounds would be affected if it did.\n\nExplain what his errors actually are, and show her that they involve sounds his tongue is making correctly in other words.\n\nBe honest that dividing a tongue that moves well would not change his speech, and say it kindly and once.\n\nExplain what is causing the unclear speech, using the pattern you found in the assessment.\n\nBe clear about what therapy involves: how many sessions, how long each block lasts, and what she does at home between them.\n\nGive her the first home activity today and practise it with her and her son in the room.\n\nExplain what progress will look like and when you would expect to see it, so she has something to judge by.\n\nAgree the plan and the review date, and tell her what would make you reconsider the tongue question."
