      "candidateRole": "You are the speech and language therapist.",
      "patientRole": "The daughter of an 88-year-old woman with advanced dementia who is eating very little and coughing at meals. The daughter has asked for a feeding tube and believes her mother is being starved.",
      "patientConcern": "She has watched her mother lose weight for a year, she feels nobody is doing anything, and she has been told different things by different staff.",
      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask her to tell you what she has seen over the past year, and what worries her most now.\n\nAsk what she has been told by other staff, so that you can address the contradiction rather than add to it.\n\nAcknowledge plainly how hard it is to watch a parent stop eating, and say that wanting to feed her is not the wrong instinct.\n\nExplain what you found on the swallow assessment, in ordinary words, and what happens at each meal.\n\nExplain honestly what a feeding tube does and does not do in advanced dementia: what the evidence shows about pneumonia, comfort and survival.\n\nExplain that food can still go into the lungs from saliva and reflux even when nothing is eaten by mouth, since this is the part families are rarely told.\n\nExplain what careful hand feeding for comfort actually means in practice, so it does not sound like giving up.\n\nTalk about the things that genuinely help: mouth care, positioning, favourite tastes, small amounts often, and unhurried company at meals.\n\nAsk what her mother would have said about this herself, and whether there is any written wish or an attorney.\n\nBe clear about who makes the decision and how, and offer to arrange a meeting with the medical team rather than closing the question here.\n\nAgree what will happen at the next meal and when you will see her again."
