      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask what she has noticed, and ask her to tell you about a day at home rather than answering a checklist.\n\nAsk who has told her to wait, and what reason she was given.\n\nAsk what languages are spoken at home and how much he hears of each, since she may have been told that is the cause.\n\nSay plainly that she was right to come and that a parent's concern is the single best predictor there is.\n\nExplain what the assessment showed, using the number of words he has and the number expected at his age.\n\nExplain the difference between understanding and talking, and where he sits on each, since parents are rarely told these are separate.\n\nBe honest about what is not yet known: that some children in his position catch up and some do not, and that nobody can tell which he is today.\n\nExplain what makes the difference in the meantime, and why waiting to find out is the one option with a cost.\n\nGive her three specific things to do at home tonight: commenting rather than questioning, waiting after she speaks, and adding one word to what he says.\n\nShow her one of them properly, with a toy, rather than describing it.\n\nAsk about the things that matter alongside: his hearing, his ears, his play, and whether he points to show her things.\n\nAgree the plan: the hearing check, the next appointment, and what she should look for in the six weeks between."
