      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Direct your first question to him and wait for him, in front of her, without commenting on what you are doing.\n\nAsk him, using yes and no questions if needed, how he thinks the last week has gone.\n\nAsk her how she is, and mean the question, since nobody has asked her since the stroke.\n\nAsk how much of the day the two of them are alone together, and whether anyone else visits.\n\nAsk what a conversation at home is like now, and what happens when he cannot find a word.\n\nRaise what you have noticed gently and without blame, and check whether she has noticed it too.\n\nExplain what happens inside a conversation when the pause is filled: that he stops trying, and why that costs him more than the frustration would.\n\nBe clear that she is not doing anything wrong and that what she is doing is what almost everyone does.\n\nTeach her one specific thing to do instead: count silently to ten, and let the silence sit.\n\nTeach a second: write the key word down, so that helping becomes something visible rather than speaking for him.\n\nAsk him what he would like her to do, and give him the time to answer even though it takes a while.\n\nAgree one thing to practise this week, at one meal a day rather than all the time, and arrange to watch them do it next visit."
