      "candidateCard": "Ask what she was told after the camera examination, and what she understood by it.\n\nAsk when the voice is at its best and at its worst across a week, including weekends and holidays.\n\nAsk what happened on the eleven days she lost it altogether, and what she did about work.\n\nCorrect that directly: explain that a normal-looking larynx working badly is a real condition with a name and a treatment.\n\nExplain in ordinary words what muscle tension does to the voice, and why it gets worse across a day.\n\nShow her the difference herself: use humming or a straw and let her hear her own voice change in the room.\n\nExplain why that response is good news, and what it predicts about therapy.\n\nAsk about the day: hours of teaching, playground duty, the choir, and how she uses her voice at each.\n\nAsk about the practical things that make it worse: how much she drinks, coffee, throat clearing, and talking over noise.\n\nDeal with throat clearing specifically, since it is the habit doing the most damage, and give her something to do instead.\n\nBe realistic about voice rest: agree what is achievable rather than telling a teacher not to speak.\n\nTalk about the workplace: amplification, non-speaking duties, and what you can put in writing for the school.\n\nAgree the home programme, how many minutes twice a day, and when you will see her again.\n\nAsk what she is most worried about at work, and be clear about what you can and cannot say on her behalf."
