      "candidateCard": "Ask him what the drinks are like and what exactly he objects to, rather than assuming it is stubbornness.\n\nAsk how much he has been drinking since the thickener started, and whether he has been thirsty.\n\nAsk whether anyone explained to him why the drinks were changed, or whether they simply arrived different.\n\nAsk what the last three weeks have been like for him, and listen to the answer.\n\nExplain what you saw when he drank water, and what happens in the lungs afterwards.\n\nCheck what he understands: ask him to tell you back, in his own words, what the risk is.\n\nBe honest about it: what a chest infection would mean for him, and that it can be serious, without using it as a threat.\n\nBe equally honest that many people make this choice, and that it is his to make if he understands it.\n\nExplore what could be changed before he decides: a different thickener, a colder drink, a stronger flavour, a different fluid altogether.\n\nExplain the things that reduce the risk whatever he drinks: sitting fully upright, small sips, a second swallow, and mouth care.\n\nExplain that mouth care matters as much as the thickener, and why, since nobody has told him that.\n\nAsk what he wants to happen, and tell him plainly what you will write down and who will read it.\n\nAgree to record his decision properly, arrange the medical review it needs, and tell him he may change his mind at any time."
