      "candidateCard": "Ask him what has changed since he signed the form, and let him say the whole of it.\n\nAsk what he was told at the consent appointment and how much of it he remembers.\n\nAsk who he has talked to since, and whether his family know how he is feeling tonight.\n\nAsk what he believes life will be like afterwards, in detail, rather than in general terms.\n\nFind out where his picture came from: a relative, something he read, or something someone said in a waiting room.\n\nCorrect what is wrong in it, plainly, before you offer anything else.\n\nExplain the three ways of speaking after a laryngectomy, in ordinary words, and what each involves.\n\nPlay him a recording, or offer to arrange for him to meet someone who has had the operation and speaks.\n\nBe honest about what will be different: the sound, the effort, the time it takes to learn, and the things that will not come back.\n\nExplain the stoma and the practical daily reality, since much of the fear is about that rather than the voice.\n\nAsk about his work and his children specifically, since those are the two things he has named.\n\nBe clear that the decision is his, that nobody will force him, and that this conversation is not about persuading him.\n\nAgree what happens next: someone to speak to, another appointment before the date, and who he can telephone tonight."
