      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask him what he has noticed himself, and ask his son separately what he has noticed.\n\nAsk when the change began, and whether anything else about his speech has altered: the rate, the clarity, or the swallowing.\n\nAsk whether he has had his hearing tested, since that should be excluded rather than assumed.\n\nAsk what happens at family meals now, and how it usually ends.\n\nExplain that in Parkinson's the sense of how loudly one is speaking becomes unreliable, and that this is part of the condition rather than a matter of effort.\n\nMeasure his volume in front of him and show him the number against the expected range.\n\nAsk him to speak at what feels far too loud to him, measure that, and let him see where it lands.\n\nExplain what that mismatch means, and why being told to speak up does not work.\n\nExplain what intensive voice therapy involves: how many sessions, how often, and the daily practice it demands.\n\nBe honest about the commitment, since a programme he will not do is worth nothing.\n\nAsk what he has stopped doing because of his voice: the telephone, the club, the grandchildren.\n\nGive the family something to change too: sitting closer, facing him, turning off the television, and not finishing his sentences.\n\nAgree whether he wants to start, and if he is unsure, agree a trial of two sessions rather than pressing for a decision today."
