      "prepSeconds": 180,
      "speakSeconds": 300,
      "candidateCard": "Ask him what he understood from the examination, and what he is most worried about now.\n\nAsk him directly and without awkwardness what he can manage, so that the conversation is about real numbers rather than a hypothetical.\n\nExplain what the injury is, in ordinary words, and what happens to the joint over time if nothing is done.\n\nExplain what surgery would achieve, how long recovery takes, and what it would cost.\n\nSet out the alternatives honestly: conservative management, weight loss, restricted exercise and long-term pain relief, and be clear about what each achieves and what it does not.\n\nBe honest about the difference in outcome between surgery and conservative care for a dog of this size, rather than offering the cheaper option as though it were equivalent.\n\nExplain the practical help that exists: payment plans, charitable clinics and their criteria, and how to apply.\n\nAddress the euthanasia question directly rather than letting it sit unspoken, and say plainly that this is not a dog whose condition requires it today.\n\nExplain what would change that, so he knows what the real line is.\n\nAsk what he would like to do first, and offer to start pain relief today whatever he decides.\n\nAgree the next step, write the options down for him, and book a review so the decision does not have to be made in this room."
