      "candidateCard": "Check first what she already knows and what she has been expecting since the scan.\n\nAsk whether she would like to telephone anyone to be with her before you begin.\n\nGive a short warning that the news is not good, then say the finding plainly in one sentence.\n\nStop and say nothing. Let her react before you add anything.\n\nAsk whether she wants the detail now or in a few minutes, and follow her answer.\n\nExplain what has been found, in ordinary words, and what it means for how long he has.\n\nBe honest about the uncertainty in that estimate rather than giving a number you cannot stand behind.\n\nExplain what the realistic options are: palliative care, chemotherapy if it applies, and what each would involve for the dog rather than for the disease.\n\nBe clear about what is not on the table, and why, so that \"everything possible\" has a real meaning.\n\nExplain what to watch for at home, particularly the signs of a bleed, and what to do at any hour.\n\nRaise quality of life and how she will judge it, and offer a simple way of tracking it week by week.\n\nAsk who she has at home, arrange a follow-up call in two days, and make sure she is not driving home straight away if she is not fit to.\n\nWrite the main points down for her, since almost nothing said after bad news is remembered."
